  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low will develop off the East Coast of\nFlorida by Friday which will pass by the Mid-Atlantic region \nSunday into Monday.\n\nHigh pressure over the Mid-Atlantic will persist through Friday\nbefore shifting offshore on Saturday. Tranquil conditions will\ncontinue with above-normal temperatures around the region. \n\nMeanwhile, some broad troughing will continue to reside over \nthe Southeast US through late week. As some upper energy arrives\nwithin the sub-tropical jet-stream, a closed low is forecast to\ndevelop and strengthen to the east of Florida on Friday. This \nlow is then progged to gradually lift northward off the coast of\nN FL/GA/SC on Saturday and near the coast of NC on Sunday.\n\nUpon evaluation of the 19/00Z guidance suite, there has been \na tighter clustering amongst available guidance including the \ndeterministic GFS, CMC, AI guidance including AIFS and AI-GFS \nand associated ensemble members. In fact, the GFS/GEFS has come \nmore into line with a slower progression of the system. The \nclustering of the track are now all within close proximity to \none another by Sunday night with the low somewhere over/near \nCape Hatteras. The lone outlier at this time is the deterministic\nECMWF which keeps the low much further south and east. Beyond \nthis point, the track of the coastal low varies depending on the\ntiming and strength of an approaching upper trough from the \nwest and how quickly the western periphery of a surface high \nover the North Atlantic recedes. AI guidance has been more \nbullish with the track keeping the low closer to the coast, \nwhere others track a bit further offshore. Depending on the \nexact track and strength, will ultimately determine the \nmagnitude of impacts to our area. In any case, PoPs continue to \nincrease now in the 40-80% range late Sunday into Monday \n(highest PoPs near the coast).\n\nThe National Hurricane Center now has a 60% chance of cyclone\nformation in the next 7 days over the western Atlantic.\nRegardless of (sub) tropical development, it is becoming \nincreasingly likely that strong winds, high seas, and coastal \nflooding will be possible late Sunday into early next week.\nWhile any rainfall will be beneficial considering recent dry\nstretch, the Weather Prediction Center has most of the area in a\nMarginal risk for Excessive Rainfall Sunday into Monday.\n\nBehind the system, a strong cold front will then cross through \nthe region on Monday, bringing the return of high pressure to \nthe region mid-week with cooler temperatures.",
